{
  "image_name": "2b2efaa45e3117d917ef5df214e5df0c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure compares the organization of neural progenitor cells and neuron production in the developing mouse and human cortex. It shows that both species contain several progenitor types in layered germinal zones, but the human cortex has a much more elaborate arrangement, especially in the outer subventricular region.\n\nIn the mouse, progenitors are mainly confined to the ventricular zone and subventricular zone, with neurons migrating outward through the intermediate zone to the cortical plate. The human diagram shows the same basic radial migration pattern but with additional complexity: the subventricular zone is split into inner and outer regions, and there are many more basal progenitors and radial glia, particularly outer/basal radial glial cells with long fibers extending toward the cortical surface. Migrating neurons travel along these elongated radial processes to populate the cortical plate.\n\nThe human panel also highlights cortical folding, contrasting a sulcus and a gyrus. The expanded outer germinal zones and abundant radial scaffolds appear associated with greater neuron production and the thicker, folded cortex. Overall, the figure emphasizes that human cortical development relies on expanded progenitor diversity and radial architecture compared with the simpler mouse cortex.",
